Neurodivergent Masking Burnout - Adjustment Disorder - Focus of clinical attention: Autism Spectrum Disorder / ADHD
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Masking exhaustion, Executive functioning collapse, Sensory overload

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-034
Reason for Visit: Acute functional collapse presenting with severe sensory overload, situational mutism, and autonomic dysregulation mimicking an acute neurological event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe cessation of verbal communication, profound photophobia, and extreme physical lethargy following a prolonged period of intense social demands. Immediate face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out a silent migraine, acute encephalopathy, or severe medication reaction prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Patient points to head and covers ears, unable to speak. Roommate states: "They haven't spoken or moved from this dark room in two days after a major work conference."]
History of Present Illness: The roommate provides the history due to the patient's situational mutism. The patient describes a history of forced neurotypical behavior at work. Over the past 48 hours, following a multi-day corporate event, the patient experienced a total collapse of executive functioning and severe physical and mental exhaustion. This led to days of necessary isolation. The patient exhibits intense sensory overload in previously tolerable environments, profound identity confusion, and a complete regression in coping mechanisms. Somatic symptoms include severe migraines, gastrointestinal shutdown (loss of appetite), and marked autonomic dysregulation. The patient demonstrates complete apathy toward previously intense special interests and a severe reluctance to re-enter any social space.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound, non-verbal, and completely dependent on the roommate for hydration and basic needs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration and occupational jeopardy due to profound, unmanaged masking burnout and executive failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 60 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is lying in a fetal position in a completely darkened room, wearing noise-canceling headphones, and rocking slightly.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Bradycardic (normal for deep rest), normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear, shallow breathing. Neurological: Fully conscious but exhibiting situational mutism (nods to questions but cannot vocalize). Cranial nerves grossly intact. No focal motor deficits. The clinical picture is dominated by masking exhaustion and sensory overload; the patient flinches visibly when the clinician uses a penlight or speaks at a normal volume. Executive functioning collapse is absolute; the patient cannot sequence the steps to drink from a water bottle without explicit, step-by-step guidance.
Environmental Observations: The room is stripped of sensory input (blinds drawn, electronics off).
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications. Has not taken any food or fluids in 12 hours.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Neurodivergent Masking Burnout - Adjustment Disorder - Focus of clinical attention: Autism Spectrum Disorder / ADHD - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Stroke / TIA (Expressive Aphasia) - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient can clearly understand complex commands and communicate accurately via text/nodding, with the mutism being strictly situational and accompanied by intense sensory avoidance, not structural brain ischemia.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, negative neck stiffness, and the gradual onset correlating exactly with a known period of intense social masking.

Catatonia - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient remains responsive to the environment (flinching, texting), rather than exhibiting waxy flexibility, posturing, or complete stupor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe psychomotor retardation, situational mutism, and profound sensory intolerance directly resulting from a massive cognitive and sensory overload (masking burnout). Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a modified neurological exam to definitively exclude an acute cerebrovascular event causing aphasia, or an infectious process like meningitis causing photophobia. Delaying care until routine hours would have risked missing a critical organic pathology and allowed dehydration to worsen due to executive collapse, firmly justifying the urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict sensory rest and assisted the roommate with guided oral rehydration. Wrote a medical certificate for 5 days of absolute stress leave. Recommended the usual GP arrange psychometric confirmation on the CAT-Q paired with documenting the parasympathetic collapse.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the executive functioning collapse safely, the roommate will implement a "binary choice protocol": instead of asking open-ended questions ("What do you want to eat?"), offer only two physical, visible options (e.g., holding up an apple or a cracker); removing the cognitive burden of decision-making preserves depleted executive energy. To recover from masking exhaustion, enforce 48 hours of "zero-demand time": no questions, no tasks, and communication strictly via text message; removing the expectation of neurotypical performance allows the nervous system to reboot.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a neurodiversity-affirming occupational therapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Continued use of noise-canceling headphones and blackout curtains.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours (via telehealth to avoid sensory exposure) to assess hydration and the return of verbal communication.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the patient becomes completely unrousable, develops a fever, or experiences sudden weakness on one side of the body.

